<title>UK: World drug sales up 7 percent in 1996 - IMS.</title>
<headline>World drug sales up 7 percent in 1996 - IMS.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-03-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Pharmaceutical sales in 10 of the world's top markets rose seven percent in 1996 in local currency terms, market researcher IMS International said on Monday.</p>
<p>In dollar terms sales rose to $143.8 billion last year from $139.15 billion in 1995.</p>
<p>IMS said sales in the U.S. rose 10 percent to $63.9 billion. Other strong performers were Italy, with sales up 11 percent in locol currency terms, and Britain, where sales rose 10 percent.</p>
<p>IMS detailed retail pharmacy sales as follows, in billion dollars, with growth rates in local currency terms -</p>
<p>		    Jan-Dec 96	    Jan-Dec 95	Percent</p>
<p>								    growth</p>
<p> U.S.	     63.95		   58.25		 10</p>
<p> Canada	    3.74		    3.56		  4</p>
<p> Germany	  16.53		   16.52		  5</p>
<p> France	   15.08		   15.20		  2</p>
<p> Italy	     8.97		    7.66		 11</p>
<p> UK		  6.82		    6.26		 10</p>
<p> Spain	     5.12		    4.77		  9</p>
<p> Netherlands     1.99		    2.08		  1</p>
<p> Belgium	   2.00		    1.99		  6</p>
<p> Japan	    23.35		   26.44		  3</p>
<p> Total	   143.79		  139.15		  7</p>
<p>By therapeutic category, IMS said the strongest growth was seen in central nervous system drugs, which grew by 14 percent, blood agents, up 16 percent, cytostatics, up 12 percent and parasitology, up 15 percent.</p>
<p>IMS detailed the top five therapeutic categories as follows, in billion dollars, with growth rates in local currency terms:</p>
<p>		   Jan-Dec 96	   Jan-Dec 95	   Percent</p>
<p>								     Growth</p>
<p> Cardiovascular  25.53		 25.56		    3</p>
<p>Alimentary/</p>
<p> Metabolism	24.59		 23.45		    9</p>
<p>Central</p>
<p> Nervous System  20.30		 18.19		    14</p>
<p> Respiratory     14.56		 14.21		     6</p>
<p> Anti-infectives 14.53		 14.69		     3</p>
<p>--London Newsroom +44 171 542 7717</p>
